course: dictionary
lesson: 1853
title: A cocoon: a case or a cosy wrap?
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show you which one is meant. «The caterpillar spun a cocoon around itself.» Here a cocoon is a silky case that an insect wraps itself in, because caterpillars spin silk.
explain: «She sat in a cocoon of blankets on the sofa.» There is no insect here. A cocoon is also any soft, warm covering that keeps you safe. Check your meaning: a warm, soft wrap fits the sentence, so it works.
explain: Cocoon can also be a verb, a doing word. «The sleeping bag cocooned him, and he felt safe.» To cocoon someone means to wrap them up and protect them. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Wrapped up fits, so that is the meaning.
fact: A cocoon is a silky case that some insect larvae spin around themselves.
fact: Moths and silkworms make cocoons, and inside them they change into adults.
fact: Silk comes from silkworm cocoons.
fact: «Cocoon» can also mean any soft, warm, safe covering, like a cocoon of blankets.
fact: «Cocooned» means wrapped up and protected from the world.
exercise: «The caterpillar became a pupa inside its cocoon.» What is a cocoon here? | A cold, hard stone | *A silky case an insect spins around itself | A tall tree in the forest | A sweet, sticky cake
exercise: «A cocoon of blankets kept her warm all night.» What does cocoon mean here? | A cold, hard shell | A loud noise | A kind of pet | *A soft, warm, safe covering
exercise: «The sleeping bag cocooned the baby.» What does cocooned mean? | Chased away | Lost in the woods | Painted in bright colours | *Wrapped up and protected
test: «A caterpillar grows, then makes a chrysalis or cocoon and comes out with wings.» What does the caterpillar become? | A fish in a pond | A bigger caterpillar | *An adult moth or butterfly | A pile of leaves
test: «Silk comes from silkworm cocoons.» Which word names the silky case that a silkworm spins? | Chrysalis | Mulberry | Lungfish | *Cocoon
exam: Which sentence uses cocoon as a verb, meaning to wrap up and protect? | «The moth spun a cocoon.» | *«She cocooned the baby in a soft towel.» | «The cocoon was pale and silky.»
